insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 1, 'In 2027 the Ekene power plant agreement (synthetic) states an ACQ of 7665000.000000 MMBtu, force majeure of 42000.000000 and a seller shortfall of 6300.000000, with take-or-pay at 80 percent. What take-or-pay quantity does the engine return for the year?', '["6132000.000000, which is 80 percent of the full ACQ before any reduction is taken off it", "7616700.000000, the Adjusted ACQ itself", "6093360.000000, which is 80 percent of the Adjusted ACQ of 7616700.000000", "6148800.000000, the leap-year quantity of 2028"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine takes the force majeure and the seller shortfall off the ACQ first (7665000.000000 less 48300.000000 is an Adjusted ACQ of 7616700.000000) and then applies 80 percent, giving 6093360.000000. 6132000.000000 is 80 percent of the ACQ with the reductions left in, which is the take-or-pay quantity of a year with nothing to reduce. The Adjusted ACQ is the base of the percentage, and at 80 percent the take-or-pay quantity sits below it. 6148800.000000 belongs to 2028, a leap year of 366 days, and each year is reconciled on its own ACQ.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 2, 'A learner builds a takeOrPay call whose years run 2027 and then 2029, leaving 2028 out. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal naming years[1].year, which in the engine''s own words must be 2028, the year after 2027 (contract years are consecutive)", "A two-year ledger in which 2028 is treated as a year with nothing taken and a full deficiency paid on its ACQ", "Two separate one-year reconciliations, each closing its own make-up account at the end of its own year", "A ledger for 2027 and 2029 in which any make-up opened in 2027 counts one extra year of its period as spent"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine refuses a list of contract years that skips a year, and its message reads: "years[1].year must be 2028, the year after 2027 (contract years are consecutive); got 2029". It invents no missing year, so there is no 2028 with a zero take. It does not split the call into one-year cases either, and it does not guess how a gap would age a make-up entry: the call is refused before anything is computed.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 3, 'The power plant takes 5460000.000000 in 2028 against a take-or-pay quantity of 6148800.000000. The fixture''s take-or-pay price of 2.180000 is the reported 2026 domestic base price held flat as a stated planning assumption. What does the engine return for the year?', '["A deficiency of 688800.000000 and a payment of 0.000000, because the make-up right opened in 2028 cancels the payment for the year", "A deficiency of 357000.000000 paid at 778260.000000", "A deficiency payment of 13404384.000000, the whole of the year''s money when the buyer falls under take-or-pay", "A deficiency of 688800.000000 and a deficiency payment of 1501584.000000, on that planning price"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The deficiency is the take-or-pay quantity less the quantity counted, 6148800.000000 less 5460000.000000, which is 688800.000000, and the engine''s reason prices it at 688800 x 2.18 = 1501584 on the fixture''s stated planning price. The make-up right does not cancel the payment: make-up is gas paid for now and taken later. 357000.000000 and 778260.000000 are the 2033 deficiency and its payment, a second entry in the same term. 13404384.000000 is the whole net to the seller for 2028, which adds the gas taken at the contract price to the deficiency payment.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 4, 'In this course''s legislated vocabulary, what is make-up?', '["Gas the seller failed to make available on a day, owed back later", "Gas paid for in a deficiency year and taken in a later year of its make-up period", "Takes above a stated base in one year, credited against a deficiency that arises in some later year", "Any quantity nominated above the MaxDCQ, supplied the next year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The vocabulary fixes make-up as gas paid for in a deficiency year and taken later, inside its make-up period. Gas the seller did not make available is a seller shortfall, which reduces the Adjusted ACQ and may carry damages; it is no make-up. Takes above a base credited against a later deficiency are carry-forward, the buyer''s other right. A nomination above the MaxDCQ is simply not properly nominated for the part above it, and no right to later gas follows from it.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 5, 'Which statement matches the engine''s own basis for the order in which make-up is recovered?', '["It defaults to ''first'' when the contract is silent, since HMRC''s manual lists make-up in priority as the usual arrangement", "It is a required input with no default; ''after-adjusted-acq'' is named as the reference text''s order and the other two are variants the engine also computes", "It is fixed at ''after-top-quantity'', since ESMAP describes that order as normal, and the engine refuses the two other orders", "It is chosen by the engine in each year as whichever of the three orders lets the buyer draw the most make-up gas"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s basis reads that ''after-adjusted-acq'' is the reference text''s order (the Commonwealth model agreement''s Article 12.7.1) and that ''after-top-quantity'' and ''first'' are variants the engine also computes, and a call without an order is refused. There is no default, so a silent contract never falls back to ''first''. ESMAP''s order is one of the three the engine computes, and it refuses none of them. The engine takes the order a contract states and never searches for the one that suits a party.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 6, 'Three golden cases run the same years under each recovery order. In 2028 the buyer has 200.000000 of make-up open, an Adjusted ACQ of 1000.000000, a take-or-pay quantity of 800.000000, and takes 1100.000000. Under ''after-adjusted-acq'', how much make-up does the engine take?', '["200.000000, since make-up starts once the year''s take-or-pay quantity of 800.000000 is taken and the whole aggregate fits", "300.000000, the whole take above the take-or-pay quantity, which is more make-up than the aggregate holds for the buyer", "0.000000, since make-up opened in 2027 cannot be drawn until the contract year after it has fully aged", "100.000000, the take above the Adjusted ACQ"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ''after-adjusted-acq'' make-up is taken only after the Adjusted ACQ of the year is taken, so 1100.000000 less 1000.000000 gives 100.000000. The figure 200.000000 is what the engine returns under ''after-top-quantity'' and ''first'' on the same years, which is why the order is a stated term. 300.000000 is more than the 200.000000 the aggregate holds, so no order can draw it. Make-up opened in 2027 is usable from 2028, the first year of its period.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 7, 'Under the ''first'' order, a buyer with 200.000000 of make-up open takes 800.000000 in 2028 against a take-or-pay quantity of 800.000000. What does the engine return for the year?', '["Make-up of 200.000000 taken first, 600.000000 counted and a new deficiency of 200.000000", "800.000000 counted and no deficiency, because the year''s whole take meets the take-or-pay quantity exactly", "Make-up of 0.000000, because under ''first'' make-up waits until the year''s own take-or-pay quantity has been taken", "Make-up of 200.000000 taken and 800.000000 counted"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ''first'' make-up is taken in priority, before the year''s own quantity, so the engine draws 200.000000 of make-up and counts only 600.000000 toward the year''s take-or-pay quantity. That leaves a deficiency of 200.000000, paid at 200 x 3 = 600, which opens a new make-up entry. Counting the whole 800.000000 ignores the make-up taken. Waiting for the take-or-pay quantity is the ''after-top-quantity'' order. Make-up gas is never counted twice: the counted quantity is the take less the make-up taken.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 8, 'A golden case opens make-up of 100.000000 from 2027 and 50.000000 from 2028, each for 3 contract years. In 2029 the buyer takes 80.000000 of make-up. In 2030 it takes 30.000000 more. Where does the engine draw the 2030 make-up from?', '["30.000000 from 2028, the newer entry, since the latest deficiency is recovered before the older ones", "15.000000 from each of the two entries, split evenly because both are still inside their make-up periods", "20.000000 from 2027 and 10.000000 from 2028", "30.000000 from 2027, the older entry, which the rule draws first whatever balance it has left open"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The model agreement''s Article 12.7 attributes make-up first in first out, so the engine drains 2027 first: 80.000000 in 2029 leaves 20.000000 of it, and the 2030 draw takes that 20.000000 and then 10.000000 from 2028. Drawing the newest entry first reverses the stated rule. An even split has no basis in the text. The 2027 entry held only 20.000000 by 2030, so 30.000000 cannot come from it alone.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 9, 'In 2029 the power plant takes 7875000.000000 against an Adjusted ACQ of 7665000.000000, with 688800.000000 of make-up open from 2028 and the order ''after-adjusted-acq''. How much make-up does the engine take?', '["688800.000000, the whole aggregate, since the take is above the year''s take-or-pay quantity of 6132000.000000", "478800.000000, what stays open", "0.000000, until the period''s last year", "210000.000000, drawn from the 2028 entry"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With make-up taken only after the Adjusted ACQ, the draw is 7875000.000000 less 7665000.000000, which is 210000.000000, and the engine''s reason reads "make-up of 210000 taken from the make-up aggregate 688800". Passing the take-or-pay quantity does not release the whole aggregate under this order. 478800.000000 is what stays open after the draw. Make-up is usable in every year of its period, from the first year after the deficiency.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 10, 'In 2030 the Ekene power plant''s take lands exactly on its Adjusted ACQ of 7665000.000000 while 478800.000000 of make-up is still open under its stated order, ''after-adjusted-acq''. How much of that make-up is drawn?', '["None, because make-up is taken only strictly above the Adjusted ACQ", "478800.000000, because the take is well above the take-or-pay quantity of 6132000.000000 and the aggregate is open", "The gap between the Adjusted ACQ and the take-or-pay quantity, which the engine draws as make-up whenever the aggregate can cover it", "Half of the open aggregate, released at the Adjusted ACQ"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s reason reads "make-up aggregate 478800 available and none taken, because taken 7665000 does not exceed the Adjusted ACQ 7665000": a take equal to the Adjusted ACQ leaves nothing above it to count as make-up. Clearing the take-or-pay quantity matters under ''after-top-quantity'', which this agreement does not state. No rule measures make-up from the gap between the two quantities, and no rule releases half an aggregate.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 11, 'A contract''s make-up terms are typed with the key makeup, all in lower case. What does the engine return?', '["A ledger run with no make-up right, the block being dropped", "A ledger run on the make-up terms, since the engine matches its keys without regard to upper or lower case", "A refusal: makeup is not an accepted key, with the accepted top-level keys listed", "A ledger run on the model agreement''s own make-up terms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses any key it does not read, and the message is its own: "makeup is not an accepted key; the accepted keys at the top level are years, topPct, makeUp, carryForward". It never drops a misspelt block silently, so no ledger runs without make-up. Keys are matched exactly, case included. The engine holds no default make-up terms to fill in, which is why a missing makeUp block is refused too.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 12, 'What quantity does the engine count toward the power plant''s 2029 take-or-pay quantity, in a year it takes 7875000.000000 and draws 210000.000000 of make-up?', '["7875000.000000, the whole take", "7665000.000000, the take less the make-up taken", "7875000.000000 plus the 210000.000000 drawn, since the draw is paid gas that the buyer has now received", "6132000.000000, the take-or-pay quantity itself, which is all the counted quantity can ever reach in a year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The counted quantity is the take less the make-up taken, 7875000.000000 less 210000.000000, which is 7665000.000000, as the ledger prints it. Counting the whole take would count the make-up gas twice, once in 2028 when it was paid for and again in 2029. Adding the draw doubles it the other way. The counted quantity is not capped at the take-or-pay quantity: in 2029 it is above it and there is no deficiency.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 13, 'The model agreement published by the Commonwealth Secretariat (2025, CC BY 4.0) states the obligation that every row of the ledger tests. Which quantity does its Article 12.6 oblige the buyer to take and pay for, or pay for if not taken, in each contract year?', '["At least the Take or Pay Quantity", "At least the Annual Contract Quantity, before the stated reductions for maintenance and force majeure", "At least the Adjusted ACQ, with the deficiency payment charged on anything the buyer leaves untaken", "At least the DCQ on every day, with each day''s buyer shortfall paid for at the end of the month"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Article 12.6 reads: "In each Contract Year Buyer shall be obligated to take and pay for, or to pay for if not taken, a quantity of Gas at least equal to the Take or Pay Quantity." The ACQ and the Adjusted ACQ are the quantities the take-or-pay quantity is built from; the obligation is the stated percentage of the Adjusted ACQ. The daily balance records buyer shortfall by day, but the payment is reconciled once a contract year.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 14, 'At the end of 2028, what does the power plant ledger carry into 2029?', '["A carry-forward credit of 688800.000000, available against any deficiency the buyer runs up later in the term", "A 2029 take-or-pay quantity raised by 688800.000000, so that the buyer takes the deficiency on top of the year", "Nothing, since the payment closes every account", "Make-up outstanding of 688800.000000, recoverable to the end of 2031"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 2028 deficiency is paid and opens as make-up: the engine''s reason reads "the buyer may make up 688800 in the 3 contract years after 2028, to the end of 2031". Carry-forward is a different right, credited from takes above a base, and the power plant agreement states none. The ledger never raises a later year''s take-or-pay quantity. The payment closes the year''s obligation and opens the make-up account; it does not close that account.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-take-or-pay-ledger', 15, 'Why does the power plant''s take-or-pay quantity read 6148800.000000 in 2028 and 6132000.000000 in 2029, with no reduction stated in either year?', '["2028 carries the deficiency that the buyer runs up that year, which raises its take-or-pay quantity above the next year''s", "2029 takes the make-up of 210000.000000 off its ACQ before the percentage of 80 percent is applied to it", "2028 is a leap year of 366 days, so its ACQ is 7686000.000000", "2028 is the year the domestic base price is re-determined, so the engine scales its ACQ by the new price"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ACQ is the DCQ times the days in the contract year: 21000.000000 over 366 days gives 7686000.000000 in 2028 and over 365 days 7665000.000000 in 2029, and 80 percent of each gives the two take-or-pay quantities. A deficiency is measured against the take-or-pay quantity and does not change it. Make-up taken reduces the counted quantity and leaves the ACQ alone. Prices do not enter the quantities at all.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 1, 'A golden case pays a deficiency of 200.000000 in 2027 under a make-up period of 2 contract years. Which is the last year in which the engine lets any of it be taken as make-up?', '["2028, counting the deficiency year as the first", "2029, the year y + N, with the rest expiring at its end", "2030, since a make-up right of N years is usable for N whole years after the period''s last year", "Any year to the end of the term"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A make-up period of N contract years after a deficiency year y runs to the end of year y + N inclusive, so 2027 plus 2 is 2029, and the engine''s reason reads "the buyer may make up 200 in the 2 contract years after 2027, to the end of 2029". The period starts in the year after the deficiency, so 2028 is its first year and 2029 its last. None is available in 2030, the year after. The period is a stated term, so it does not run on to the end of the term.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 2, 'In the golden case whose make-up of 200.000000 from 2027 reaches the last year of its 2 year period, the buyer takes 100.000000 of make-up in 2029. What does the engine return for that year?', '["100.000000 taken, with the other 100.000000 still open for 2030 because the period ends on its last day", "0.000000 taken, because make-up is never usable in the last year of its own period under the stated terms", "200.000000 taken, the whole entry", "100.000000 taken, and 100.000000 from 2027 expired at the end of 2029"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Make-up is usable in the last year of its period and the rest expires at that year''s end: the engine''s reasons read "make-up of 100 taken from the make-up aggregate 200" and "make-up of 100 from 2027 expired unrecovered at the end of 2029, the last year of its make-up period". Nothing of the entry reaches 2030. The last year is inside the period, so a draw in it is allowed. The draw is limited by the take above the Adjusted ACQ, so the last year releases nothing extra.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 3, 'A buyer who paid a deficiency of 200.000000 in 2027, with a make-up period of 2 contract years and the order ''after-adjusted-acq'', takes nothing above its Adjusted ACQ in 2028 or 2029 and plans to recover the gas in 2030. What does the engine hold for it in 2030?', '["Nothing: the whole 200.000000 expired at the end of 2029", "200.000000, available in 2030 because the entry never had a year with a take above the Adjusted ACQ to draw on", "Half of the unused entry, rolling into the year after its period", "200.000000 as a carry-forward credit, which the engine converts from any make-up that goes unused"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s reason reads "make-up of 200 from 2027 expired unrecovered at the end of 2029, the last year of its make-up period", and in 2030 the entry holds 0.000000. A year with no take above the Adjusted ACQ does not extend the period. No share of an expired entry rolls on. Make-up and carry-forward are separate rights; unused make-up never turns into a credit.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 4, 'The same deficiency of 200.000000 from 2027 is run with a make-up period of 1 contract year. In 2028 the take above the Adjusted ACQ allows 50.000000 of make-up. What does the engine return for 2028?', '["50.000000 taken and 150.000000 still open until the end of 2029, one year after the draw", "0.000000 taken, since a period of 1 contract year gives the buyer no year of make-up at all to draw in", "50.000000 drawn; the other 150.000000 lapses as 2028 closes", "200.000000 taken, the whole entry at once"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With N = 1 the period is 2028 alone, the year after the deficiency: the engine draws 50.000000 and its reason reads "make-up of 150 from 2027 expired unrecovered at the end of 2028, the last year of its make-up period". A draw does not restart the period. A period of 1 contract year is one usable year. A draw is limited by the take above the Adjusted ACQ, so the whole entry cannot be forced through.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 5, 'A contract states a make-up period of 0 years, and the buyer falls 200.000000 short in 2027 at a take-or-pay price of 3. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal, since a make-up period below 1 year is outside the integers the engine accepts for the term", "No deficiency payment at all", "Payment of 600.000000 with no make-up right arising", "A deficiency payment of 600.000000 with the make-up right carried to the end of the term by default"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A period of 0 years is accepted (the refusal is for a non-integer or a negative figure) and the engine''s reason reads "the deficiency payment is 200 x 3 = 600; the make-up period is 0 years, so no make-up right arises". The deficiency payment follows from the take-or-pay quantity whatever the period says, so it is still paid. The engine holds no default period to fall back on.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 6, 'In 2031 the Ekene power plant (synthetic) takes 268800.000000 of make-up from an aggregate of 478800.000000 opened by its 2028 deficiency, under a make-up period of 3 contract years. What happens to the remaining 210000.000000?', '["It expires unrecovered at the end of 2031, the last year of its period", "It carries into 2032 as make-up", "It is refunded to the buyer at the 2031 take-or-pay price, because the agreement''s make-up terms name a refund", "It stays open until 2034"]'::jsonb, 0, '2028 plus 3 is 2031, so the engine''s reason reads "make-up of 210000 from 2028 expired unrecovered at the end of 2031, the last year of its make-up period". Expiry at the end of the period comes before any end-of-term rule, which acts only on entries still open when the term ends. The power plant states forfeit at the end of the term; it is the export feed agreement that states a refund.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 7, 'The 210000.000000 of make-up that expires at the end of 2031 was paid for in the power plant''s 2028 deficiency payment, which rests on the fixture''s stated planning price. What does its expiry do to the money?', '["The 2028 payment for it is reversed", "The seller owes damages at the stated seller shortfall price", "It becomes a carry-forward credit for the buyer", "The seller keeps what was paid, and the buyer loses the right to take that gas"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A deficiency payment buys the right to take the gas later; when an entry expires the right ends and nothing is paid back, which is why the power plant ledger shows 210000.000000 in makeUpExpired and no refund. No payment is reversed. Seller shortfall damages price gas the seller did not make available, which is a different event. Carry-forward is built only from takes above a base, and expired make-up does not feed it.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 8, 'A golden case ends its term in 2029 with 150.000000 of make-up still open from 2027, under the end-of-term rule ''refund''. The take-or-pay price is 2.5 in 2027 and 4 in 2029. What does the engine refund?', '["The 150.000000 at the 2027 take-or-pay price of 2.5, the price at which the buyer first paid for it", "600.000000, the 150.000000 at the last year''s price of 4", "500.000000, the whole of the 2027 deficiency payment returned to the buyer", "1000.000000, which adds the 2029 deficiency payment of 400.000000 to the refund of the earlier make-up"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s reason reads "the delivery period ends with make-up of 150 unrecovered; the seller refunds 150 x 4 = 600", at the take-or-pay price of the last contract year, as the model agreement''s Alternative 2 prints it. The deficiency year''s price is not the basis of the refund. 500.000000 was the 2027 payment on 200.000000, and 50.000000 of that gas was already taken. The last year''s own deficiency payment stays with the seller.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 9, 'The Commonwealth Secretariat''s model agreement (2025, CC BY 4.0) prints alternatives for make-up left at the end of the Delivery Period. At which price does its Alternative 2 have the seller pay the Make-Up Aggregate back?', '["The Take or Pay Price in the Contract Year in which the Delivery Period expires", "The deficiency year''s own Take or Pay Price", "The average Contract Price over the term", "The domestic base price reported for the year the term ends"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Alternative 2 reads: "Seller shall pay to Buyer an amount equal to the value of the Make-Up Aggregate multiplied by the Take or Pay Price in the Contract Year in which the Delivery Period expires." The deficiency years'' prices and a term average do not enter it. The domestic base price is a Nigerian sector price and a stated input; a make-up refund is a contract term priced by the contract.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 10, 'In the last contract year of the same refund case, 2029, the buyer counts 700.000000 against a take-or-pay quantity of 800.000000 and pays 400.000000. What does the engine''s stated reading do with that year''s own deficiency of 100.000000?', '["A make-up right refunded at once, raising the refund to 1000.000000", "Added to the aggregate and forfeited", "It is refunded at the 2029 price of 4", "Under the engine''s stated reading it opens no make-up right, so the refund covers the 2027 entry only"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine states its reading in its own words, "a last-contract-year deficiency creates no make-up right (forfeit/refund applies to earlier years'' make-up only)", and its reason for 2029 reads "the delivery period ends with this year, so no make-up right arises". This is the engine''s stated choice where the text leaves the point open. On that reading nothing from 2029 enters the aggregate, so there is nothing of it to refund or forfeit, and the refund stays at 600.000000 for the 150.000000 from 2027.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 11, 'In 2034, the last year of the power plant agreement, the buyer takes 105000.000000 of make-up from an aggregate of 357000.000000 opened in 2033. The fixture states ''forfeit'' at the end of the term. What does the engine return?', '["252000.000000 refunded at the 2034 take-or-pay price, since an entry that is still inside its period is repaid", "252000.000000 forfeited when the term ends", "252000.000000 kept open to the end of 2036, the end of the 2033 entry''s own period", "357000.000000 forfeited, because make-up is not drawn in a final year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s reasons read "make-up of 105000 taken from the make-up aggregate 357000" and "the delivery period ends with make-up of 252000 unrecovered; the buyer forfeits it". The end-of-term rule is the stated term ''forfeit'', so there is no refund. The contract ends in 2034 whatever the entry''s own period says. Make-up is drawn in the last year like any other: 105000.000000 of it was taken.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 12, 'The power plant''s 2033 deficiency opens make-up to the end of 2036 though the agreement ends in 2034. Which engine behaviour settles the part of that entry still open when the term ends?', '["The make-up period, which keeps the entry open after the term until 2036, the last year of the period", "A refusal, since a make-up period that runs past the last contract year is an input the engine does not accept", "The stated end-of-term rule, which here forfeits it", "The model agreement''s term extension"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An entry''s period can run past the term; when the delivery period ends, the stated rule acts on what is still open, and the power plant states forfeit, so 252000.000000 is forfeited in 2034. No year after the term exists in the ledger, so the entry cannot stay open. The engine accepts the case and reports the forfeit. Extending the term is not one of the rules the engine accepts: makeUp.endOfTerm is ''forfeit'' or ''refund''.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 13, 'Over 2027 to 2034 the power plant pays for 1045800.000000 of deficiency. How does the engine''s ledger account for all of it by the end of the term?', '["583800.000000 taken as make-up, 210000.000000 expired and 252000.000000 forfeited", "688800.000000 taken as make-up and 357000.000000 forfeited, each year''s deficiency closed as a single block of gas", "1045800.000000 taken as make-up, since every paid quantity is recovered before the term ends", "583800.000000 taken as make-up and the rest refunded at the take-or-pay price of 2034 when the term ends"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The totals read makeUpTaken 583800.000000, makeUpExpired 210000.000000 and endOfTermQuantity 252000.000000, which sum to the 1045800.000000 paid. Entries are drawn and expired in parts, so neither deficiency closes as one block: 268800.000000 of the 2028 entry was taken in 2031 and 210000.000000 of it expired. Not every paid quantity is recovered. The power plant states forfeit, so nothing is refunded.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 14, 'A learner types the end-of-term rule as "extend", hoping to carry open make-up past the last contract year. What does the engine return?', '["A ledger that keeps open entries alive", "A ledger that forfeits the open make-up, since forfeit is what the engine applies to any rule it does not know", "A ledger with a refund at the last year''s price", "A refusal naming makeUp.endOfTerm, which must be \"forfeit\" or \"refund\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s message reads "makeUp.endOfTerm must be one of "forfeit", "refund"; got "extend"". The model agreement prints a third alternative that extends the term, and the engine does not model it, so it refuses the input by name. It never substitutes forfeit or refund for a rule it cannot read, since every end-of-term figure depends on the stated rule.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-make-up-expiry-and-the-end-of-the-term', 15, 'A learner types a make-up period of 1.5 years. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["A ledger with the period rounded up to 2 years", "makeUp.periodYears must be an integer at or above 0; got 1.5", "A ledger with the period cut to 1 year", "makeUp.periodYears must be an integer at or above 1; got 1.5, since a period below 1 year is refused"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine refuses the period by name, and its message is "makeUp.periodYears must be an integer at or above 0; got 1.5". It never rounds or truncates a stated term, since the last year of every entry depends on it. A period of 0 years is accepted and means no make-up right, so the refusal''s floor is 0; the message about a floor of 1 belongs to the carry-forward period, a different term.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 1, 'A golden contract states no carry-forward right. The buyer counts 1100.000000 in 2027 and 1050.000000 in 2028 against an Adjusted ACQ of 1000.000000, then falls 100.000000 short of its take-or-pay quantity in 2029. What does the engine charge for 2029?', '["A credit of 100.000000 from the 2027 excess, which leaves nothing to pay", "A credit of 50.000000 from 2028, at the half cap the engine applies when no cap is stated", "Nothing, because the excess takes of the two earlier years stand in as make-up for 2029", "The full 100.000000, paid at 300.000000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Carry-forward is off unless the contract states it; the engine''s basis for this case reports carry-forward off with no right stated, and its reason for 2029 reads "the deficiency payment is 100 x 3 = 300". No credit is drawn from the earlier excess, and the engine holds no default cap to apply. Excess takes are never make-up, which is gas paid for in a deficiency year and taken later.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 2, 'Two golden cases state carry-forward for 2 contract years with a cap of 100 percent, one above the Adjusted ACQ of 1000.000000 and one above the take-or-pay quantity of 800.000000. The buyer counts 1100.000000 in 2027. What surplus does each record?', '["The same 100.000000 in both cases", "Surplus of 100.000000 against 1000.000000, and of 300.000000 against 800.000000", "300.000000 above the Adjusted ACQ and 100.000000 above the take-or-pay quantity, the larger base giving the larger surplus", "1100.000000 in both, since the whole take of an over-taking year becomes the surplus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The surplus is the quantity counted above the stated base: 1100.000000 less 1000.000000 is 100.000000 on the Adjusted ACQ, and 1100.000000 less 800.000000 is 300.000000 on the take-or-pay quantity, as the engine''s reasons print. The base is a stated term, so the two cases differ. A higher base leaves a smaller surplus. Only the part above the base is credited; the rest of the take has already met the year''s own obligation.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 3, 'Under a base set at the take-or-pay quantity, surpluses of 300.000000 (2027) and 250.000000 (2028) are carried for 2 contract years. When 2029 counts 700.000000 against 800.000000, what does the ledger show?', '["A credit of 100.000000 from 2027 leaves nothing to pay, and the other 200.000000 from 2027 expires unused at the end of 2029", "A credit of 100.000000 from 2028, the newer surplus", "A credit of 300.000000, the whole 2027 surplus", "A deficiency payment of 300.000000, credits waiting for 2030"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Credits are drawn first in first out, so the 2029 deficiency of 100.000000 is met from 2027, and a 2027 surplus carried for 2 contract years lasts to the end of 2029: the engine''s reasons read "a carry-forward credit of 100 (at most 100% of the deficiency, first in first out: 100 from 2027) leaves 0" and "carry-forward of 200 from 2027 expired unused at the end of 2029". Drawing 2028 first reverses the order. A credit is never larger than the deficiency it meets. Credits apply in the year of the deficiency.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 4, 'Suppose the credit is capped at half of any deficiency. Carrying 100.000000 of excess from 2027 for 2 contract years, the buyer comes up 100.000000 below its take-or-pay quantity in 2029, priced at 3. What credit and payment result?', '["A credit of 100.000000 and a deficiency payment of 0.000000, since the carried surplus covers the whole deficiency", "A credit of 50.000000 and a deficiency payment of 300.000000, because a capped credit reduces make-up and leaves the payment whole", "A credit of 50.000000 and a deficiency payment of 150.000000", "No credit at all, since a cap of 50 percent only lets the buyer draw on surplus older than two years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The cap is a percentage of the year''s deficiency, so at 50 percent the credit is 50.000000 and the payment is 50 x 3 = 150, as the engine''s reason prints. Crediting the whole 100.000000 ignores the cap, which is what binds here. The credit comes off the deficiency before it is priced, so the payment falls to 150.000000. The cap limits the size of the credit, and says nothing about the age of the surplus.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 5, 'On the Ekene export feed ledger (synthetic), 2029 counts 14257000.000000 against a take-or-pay quantity of 20695500.000000, with 3913300.000000 of carry-forward available and a cap of 50 percent. What credit does the engine apply?', '["3219250.000000, half the deficiency of 6438500.000000", "3913300.000000, the whole carry-forward available", "2299500.000000, the 2027 surplus alone", "6438500.000000, which wipes out the deficiency for the year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The deficiency is 20695500.000000 less 14257000.000000, 6438500.000000, and the cap allows at most 50 percent of it, 3219250.000000, drawn first in first out, which the engine''s reason prints as "2299500 from 2027, 919750 from 2028". The 3913300.000000 available is more than the cap lets the year use. The 2027 surplus alone is less than the credit, so the draw continues into 2028. A credit equal to the whole deficiency is what a cap of 100 percent would allow.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 6, 'The export feed''s 2029 take-or-pay price is 9.808450, the annual average of the monthly contract prices (the model agreement''s Article 15.2.6 Alternative 1). After the carry-forward credit, what deficiency payment does the engine return?', '["45129551.627500, which is the deficiency payment the engine returns for the whole ten-year term of the agreement", "171414924.312500, the export feed''s net to the seller for 2029 with the gas taken and the payment added together", "9081070.425000, the payment of 2035", "31575852.662500"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine prices only the deficiency left after the credit: 3219250.000000 at 9.808450, and its reason reads "the deficiency payment is 3219250 x 9.80845 = 31575852.6625", quoted as the field 31575852.662500. 45129551.627500 is the deficiency payment summed over the whole term, of which 2029 is one year. 171414924.312500 is the year''s net to the seller, which adds the gas taken at the contract price. 9081070.425000 is the payment for the 2035 deficiency, after that year''s own credit.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 7, 'What make-up right does the engine open from the export feed''s 2029 deficiency, and on what basis does it say so?', '["6438500.000000, the whole deficiency, since the model agreement''s Make-Up Aggregate sums the deficiency before a credit", "3219250.000000, on the engine''s stated reading that the make-up right equals the deficiency actually paid after any carry-forward credit", "3219250.000000, which is the law under the model agreement", "None, because a year that draws a credit opens no make-up"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine states its reading in its own words: "make-up right equals the deficiency actually paid after any carry-forward credit", and its 2029 reason reads "the buyer may make up 3219250 in the 5 contract years after 2029, to the end of 2034". The model agreement''s Make-Up Aggregate sums the deficiency quantities before any credit, which is why the course teaches this figure as the engine''s stated choice and keys it only as that. Calling it the law of the model agreement misstates it. A year with a credit still pays for part of its deficiency, and that part opens make-up.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 8, 'Excess of 100.000000 from 2027 may be credited for 2 contract years. The buyer meets its take-or-pay quantity in 2028 and 2029 and first misses it, by 100.000000, in 2030, the final year. How does the engine treat 2030?', '["A credit of 100.000000 in 2030, since a surplus waits for the first deficiency whenever it comes in the term", "A credit of 50.000000 in 2030", "Its credit lapsed when 2029 closed, leaving the full 100.000000 to pay in 2030", "No payment in 2030, since a last-year deficiency is settled by the end-of-term rule"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A surplus carried for 2 contract years from 2027 lasts to the end of 2029, and the engine''s reason reads "carry-forward of 100 from 2027 expired unused at the end of 2029"; its 2030 reason reads "the deficiency payment is 100 x 3 = 300". A surplus has a stated period and does not wait. The case''s cap is 100 percent, so no half credit applies. The end-of-term rule acts on make-up already open; the year''s own deficiency is still paid.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 9, 'On the export feed ledger, carry-forward is stated for 3 contract years. In 2031 the engine reports that 694050.000000 of carry-forward expired unused. Which year''s surplus was it?', '["2027''s, the oldest surplus", "2028''s, whose period of 3 contract years ends in 2031", "2030''s", "2029''s, the outage year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A surplus from 2028 carried for 3 contract years runs to the end of 2031, and the engine''s reason reads "carry-forward of 694050 from 2028 expired unused at the end of 2031". The 2027 surplus of 2299500.000000 was drawn in full in 2029. 2029 and 2030 record no surplus at all. A credit drawn does not create a surplus; only takes above the base do.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 10, 'In 2027 the Ekene power plant''s seller made 6300.000000 fewer MMBtu available than was properly nominated, and the fixture states liquidated damages of 1.25 US$ per MMBtu not made available (a synthetic contract term). What does the engine return for that seller shortfall?', '["7875.000000, added to the seller''s net as a charge on the buyer for gas it could not take", "Nothing, because the seller shortfall already reduces the Adjusted ACQ, which is the buyer''s whole remedy for it", "6300.000000 of make-up for the buyer", "7875.000000, paid to the buyer and taken off the seller''s net"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s reason reads "seller shortfall 6300 reduces the Adjusted ACQ and is paid to the buyer at 1.25: 7875". The damages run from seller to buyer, so they reduce the net to the seller. The reduction of the Adjusted ACQ and the damages are two separate consequences, and the fixture states both. Make-up is the buyer''s right after a deficiency it paid; a seller shortfall creates none.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 11, 'A learner states a seller shortfall of 5 in a contract year and leaves the seller shortfall price out. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal: years[0].shortfallPrice must be stated, since the engine holds no default rate", "Damages at US$3.50 per MMBtu", "Damages at the year''s take-or-pay price", "No damages, with the seller shortfall still taken off the Adjusted ACQ for the year as the contract states"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s message reads "years[0].shortfallPrice must be stated when sellerShortfall is above 0 (5); the engine holds no default rate; got nothing". US$3.50 per MMBtu is the Act''s Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation penalty, a different rule on a different party. The take-or-pay price is no stand-in either. The engine does not run the year without the rate, since the net to the seller depends on it.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 12, 'In a golden year with an ACQ of 1000.000000, reductions of 200.000000 including a seller shortfall of 50.000000, 600.000000 counted, take-or-pay at 80 percent, prices of 3 and a seller shortfall price of 1.5, what net to the seller does the engine return?', '["2400.000000, the take-or-pay quantity of a year with nothing reduced, priced at 3", "120.000000, the deficiency payment alone", "1845.000000, after the damages of 75.000000", "The gas taken plus the deficiency payment, with the damages of 75.000000 added on top as a charge to the buyer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine builds the net as the gas counted at the contract price plus the deficiency payment less the seller shortfall damages: 600 x 3 plus 40 x 3, less 50 x 1.5, which is 1845.000000, and its reason reads "seller shortfall 50 reduces the Adjusted ACQ and is paid to the buyer at 1.5: 75". The deficiency payment of 120.000000 is only one term of the net. 2400.000000 is what the same prices give when nothing reduces the Adjusted ACQ and the take-or-pay quantity is exactly met. The damages run to the buyer, so they come off the net.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 13, 'The export feed''s term ends in 2036 with 457950.000000 of make-up unrecovered, refunded at that year''s take-or-pay price. The engine''s reason prints the refund as the full double it holds, with a long tail of digits after the third decimal. Which figure does the course quote and reason with?', '["The longer figure in the engine''s reason, since the reason carries the double the engine holds", "The refund rounded to cents, since it is paid in money", "3700831.335000 plus the 2069500.000000 of carry-forward still outstanding, which the refund also pays out", "3700831.335000, the numeric field"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course quotes the numeric field at six decimals, 3700831.335000, and quotes the reason only verbatim as the engine''s words, where the figure is the shortest round-trip decimal of the double. Rounding to cents is not how the course prints a figure. Carry-forward outstanding at the end of the term is not refunded: it is a credit against deficiencies, and with no year left it simply lapses.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 14, 'In the engine''s basis for the export feed ledger, what is the carry-forward cap a percentage of?', '["The year''s deficiency", "The annual quantity", "The surplus carried", "The take-or-pay quantity of the year the surplus arose in, which fixes the size of every later credit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s basis reads "surplus above the take-or-pay quantity is credited against later deficiencies, at most 50% of a year''s deficiency", which is how the 2029 credit comes to 3219250.000000 on a deficiency of 6438500.000000. ESMAP (1993, para 6.62) describes a cap stated as a percentage of the annual quantity, a different base. A cap on the surplus would limit what is carried; this cap limits what a year may use. The take-or-pay quantity is not the base of the cap.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-carry-forward-and-seller-shortfall', 15, 'A learner states carryForward.capPct as 120, hoping to let one year''s credit exceed its deficiency. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["A ledger with the cap held at 100 percent, the most a credit can ever be worth against a year''s deficiency", "carryForward.capPct must be a number from 0 to 100; got 120", "A ledger whose credit exceeds the deficiency, paying the difference to the buyer", "carryForward.cap is not an accepted key"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine refuses the cap by name: "carryForward.capPct must be a number from 0 to 100; got 120". It never clamps a stated term to a limit of its own, and a credit is never paid out beyond the deficiency it meets. The message about carryForward.cap is the engine''s refusal of a misspelt key, which is a different input.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 1, 'A golden escalated price starts at 2 in 2027-04 and escalates by 2.5 percent a year, with no rounding. What price does the engine return for 2028-04?', '["2.000000, since the price steps only when a new calendar year begins in January", "2.037500", "2.050000, the first step", "2.101250, since a year of escalation is applied again at every monthly reset"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Escalation steps in whole years on each anniversary of the base month, so 2028-04 is the first step: 2 raised by 2.5 percent is 2.050000, as the engine prints. The step follows the base month, so it comes in April and the calendar year plays no part. 2.037500 is the engine''s 2028 annual average, three months at 2.000000 and nine at 2.050000. 2.101250 is the price after the second anniversary, in 2029-04.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 2, 'For the same escalated price, what does the engine return as the 2028 annual average price, the basis of the model agreement''s Article 15.2.6 Alternative 1?', '["2.050000, the price of the last month of 2028, since the annual row reports the price that stands at the year end", "2.037500, the mean of the twelve monthly prices of 2028", "2.000000, the price that stands in January", "2.075625, which is the mean the engine returns across the 2029 months after the second anniversary"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The annual average is the arithmetic mean of the calendar year''s monthly prices: three months at 2.000000 and nine at 2.050000 give 2.037500. 2.050000 is the last month''s price, the Alternative 2 figure the engine also returns. January alone is one month of twelve. 2.075625 is the 2029 average, a year later.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 3, 'A golden oil-indexed formula prices 0.8 + 0.1485 x jcc with no floor and no ceiling, averaging over 1 month with no lag. What does the engine return in the month the jcc index is 30?', '["4.500000, a stated ceiling", "4.141250, a lower index", "6.740000, a higher index", "5.255000, on the straight line of the formula"]'::jsonb, 3, '0.8 plus 0.1485 x 30 is 5.255000, and with no floor or ceiling every month sits on the straight line. 4.500000 is what the same formula returns when a ceiling of 4.5 is stated, which this case does not state. 4.141250 is the price at a jcc of 22.5. 6.740000 is the price at a jcc of 40.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 4, 'The same formula is run with a floor of 3.5 and a ceiling of 4.5. In the month the jcc index is 10, the line gives 2.285000. What does the engine return?', '["3.500000, labelled clamped at the floor", "2.285000, since a floor applies only to an averaged index and this month averages a single value over one month", "4.500000, since the ceiling and the floor are applied together and the higher of the two bounds is the one returned", "A refusal, since the formula falls below its floor and a stated floor must hold in every month the engine prices"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine holds the price at the floor when the line falls below it and labels the month clamped: floor, as the golden case prints. The floor applies to the price whatever the averaging window. A ceiling acts only when the line rises above it. A month below the floor is a result held at the floor with its label; the engine does not refuse it.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 5, 'A golden formula prices 1 + 0.1 x oil, averaging over 6 months with a lag of 1 month and a reset every 3 months. Which window does the engine average for the price of 2025-08?', '["2025-03 to 2025-08, six months ending in the priced month itself", "2025-02 to 2025-07, six months ending one month before", "2025-01 to 2025-06, six months ending two months before 2025-08, the lag counted from the month before", "2025-07 alone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine averages averagingMonths months ending lagMonths months before the delivery month, so six months ending in 2025-07: 2025-02 to 2025-07, an average of 67.000000 and a price of 7.700000. Ending in the priced month is a lag of 0. Ending in 2025-06 would be a lag of 2. A single month is an averaging window of 1.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 6, 'With a one-month window read three months back (a lag of 3), the golden line 1 + 0.1 x oil sees oil at 60, 62, 64 and 66 from 2025-01 to 2025-04. Which 2025-04 price comes out?', '["7.000000, from the 2025-01 index of 60", "7.600000, from the 2025-04 reading of 66", "7.400000, from the 2025-03 index", "7.200000, from 2025-02"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A lag of 3 months with a window of 1 month reads the single month three months before the delivery month, 2025-01, so the price is 1 + 0.1 x 60 = 7.000000, as the engine prints. The 2025-04 index would be a lag of 0. 2025-03 and 2025-02 would be lags of 1 and 2.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 7, 'The 6 month, lag 1 formula resets every 3 months, the first block starting at 2025-08. What does the engine return for 2025-10 and 2025-11?', '["A fresh window each month", "7.700000 in both months, the price being held for six months to match the length of the averaging window", "7.700000 in 2025-10, then 8.300000 in 2025-11", "8.300000 in both months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every month of a block carries the price of the block''s first month, counted from the first priced month, so 2025-08 to 2025-10 carry 7.700000 and 2025-11 starts a new block on the window 2025-05 to 2025-10, an average of 73.000000 and a price of 8.300000. A monthly recompute is a reset of 1. The reset is its own term and is not tied to the window length. 2025-10 still sits in the first block.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 8, 'The Ekene export feed (synthetic) prices 0.5 + 0.12 x oil on the index averaged over 6 months ending 1 month before the priced month. What does the engine return for January 2027?', '["71.461667 on 2026-10 to 2027-03, priced 9.075400, the window the first quarter of 2027 closes on", "71.560000, the index for January 2027 itself, priced at once in the month the gas is delivered to the buyer", "The average of the whole 2026 index year, priced on the calendar year before the first contract year", "76.925000 on 2026-07 to 2026-12, priced 9.731000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The window is six months ending one month before January 2027, so July to December 2026, which averages 76.925000; 0.5 + 0.12 x 76.925000 is 9.731000, as the engine prints. 71.461667 and 9.075400 belong to April 2027, the second quarterly block. The January 2027 index itself is outside its own window under a lag of 1. The window is six months long, and the calendar year plays no part in it.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 9, 'Under the rounding rule ''model-gsa-4dp'', the golden formula prices an index of 11.23459 one for one. What price does the engine return?', '["11.234500, since the fifth decimal is cut off", "11.234590, the unrounded figure", "11.234300, since the rule reads the whole remainder", "11.234600"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The model agreement''s Article 15.4 rounds the final price to four decimals, and "if the number in the fifth decimal place is five (5) or more then the number in the fourth decimal place shall be rounded up": the fifth decimal of 11.23459 is 9, so the price is 11.234600. Cutting the fifth decimal is truncation. The unrounded figure is what ''none'' returns. 11.234300 is the engine''s price for a different index, 11.234346.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 10, 'The Oxford Institute for Energy Studies paper NG 175 (2022) prints a hub-indexed form, 1.15 x HH plus a constant. On a golden case of 1.15 x hh + 2.25, what does the engine return in the month hh is 3?', '["5.125000, since the hub price is read one month late", "5.700000", "6.275000, at 3.5", "The hub term alone, since the constant is added only to an oil-indexed formula"]'::jsonb, 1, '1.15 x 3 plus 2.25 is 5.700000, as the engine prints for that month. 5.125000 is the price at an hh of 2.5, the month before, and this case states no lag. 6.275000 is the price at an hh of 3.5. The constant belongs to the hub-indexed formula as stated, so it is added.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 11, 'A golden basket formula states index floors of fo 410 with weights of 0.6 for fo and 0.4 for cpi. For 2026-01 the fo average is 400.000000 and the cpi average 101.500000. What price does the engine return?', '["5.105000, with fo held at its floor of 410.000000", "5.030000, the price of the same month with no index floor stated in the contract", "5.085000, the price of the month before", "A refusal, since an index average below its stated floor is not an input the basket formula accepts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An index floor holds the index before the formula uses it: fo is held at 410.000000 and the engine returns 5.105000, where the same month with no floors prices 5.030000. 5.085000 is the 2025-12 price, where fo averages 410.000000 already. An index below its floor is a result held at the floor; the engine refuses nothing here.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 12, 'A learner prices 2025-03 on a 6 month window with a lag of 1, and the index series starts only in 2025-01. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["A price averaged over the two months the series does hold, 2025-01 and 2025-02, since a partial window is averaged", "A price of 0.000000 for 2025-03, the month the window is missing from", "months must cover the averaging window 2024-09 to 2025-02 for the price of 2025-03; got no value for 2024-09, 2024-10, 2024-11, 2024-12", "months[1].month must be 2025-02"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses a window the series does not cover, naming the missing months: its message is "months must cover the averaging window 2024-09 to 2025-02 for the price of 2025-03; got no value for 2024-09, 2024-10, 2024-11, 2024-12". It never averages a partial window, and it does not price the month at zero. The message about months[1].month is its refusal of a gap in the series, a different fault.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 13, 'Priced month by month, the export feed''s 2029 averages 9.808450 and ends December at 10.674000. A contract adopting the first alternative the model agreement prints for its take-or-pay price uses which figure?', '["The annual average, 9.808450", "The last month''s price, 10.674000, which Alternative 1 takes as the price that stands when the year closes", "The higher, 10.674000", "A mean of the two"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Alternative 1 reads "the arithmetic average of the Contract Price for each Month during such Contract Year", which is 9.808450 for 2029, and the export feed fixture states that basis. The last month''s price is Alternative 2. The model prints no rule that takes the higher figure or a mean of the two.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 14, 'The export feed fixture states price reopeners in 2031-01 and 2035-01. What does the engine return for them?', '["A new formula from each reopener month, set by the change in the oil index since the last review", "Held flat until the parties agree", "A refusal, since a reopener needs a stated outcome before the engine can price the months after it", "Each month reported only, with no outcome modelled"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine reports each reopener in its own words: "price reopener 2031-01: reported only; the engine does not model the outcome of a price review". It sets no new formula and holds no price flat, since both would be outcomes. It does not refuse a reopener; it refuses only a reopener that is not a month written ''YYYY-MM''.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-price-formulas', 15, 'A learner states the rounding rule as "4dp". What does the engine return?', '["Four-decimal rounding, half up", "Refused by name: rounding accepts only \"none\" or \"model-gsa-4dp\"", "No rounding at all", "Prices rounded to four decimals by truncation, since the unknown rule falls back to cutting off the fifth decimal place"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s message reads "rounding must be one of "none", "model-gsa-4dp"; got "4dp"". It reads no rule it does not know, so it neither applies the model agreement''s rule on a guess nor drops rounding silently, and it has no truncation rule to fall back on.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 1, 'A learner asks the engine for a power sector price under the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 and leaves the domestic base price out. What does the engine return?', '["It refuses the call: no base price is held in the engine", "The figure reported for 2026, which the engine holds as its default until the Authority publishes the next one", "The US$0.90 per MMBtu floor", "A price of 0.000000 for the power sector"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s message reads "domesticBasePrice must be stated in US$ per MMBtu: the Authority determines it each year (PIA s.167(1)) and the engine holds no default; got nothing". The figures for 2026 and 2025 are reported by the press and a law firm, and the regulator''s circular was not read, so the engine holds none of them as a default. The US$0.90 floor belongs to the gas based industries under s.168(2). The engine never prices at zero in place of a missing term.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 2, 'Which rule does the engine state for the power sector price, reading the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 s.167(5)?', '["The base price plus US$0.50 per MMBtu", "A negotiated price capped at the base", "power sector price = domestic base price, priced at the marketable natural gas delivery point", "The Fourth Schedule formula, floored"]'::jsonb, 2, 's.167(5) reads "The price of marketable natural gas applicable to the power sector shall be the domestic base price at the marketable natural gas delivery point.", and the engine''s rule is its own words, "power sector price = domestic base price", with the basis "prices at the marketable natural gas delivery point". The US$0.50 adder is the commercial sector''s, under s.167(6). A negotiated price with a ceiling is the gas distributors'' rule under s.167(7). The Fourth Schedule formula and its floor price the gas based industries.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 3, 'A call states the domestic base price reported for 2026, 2.180000 US$ per MMBtu, and asks for the commercial sector price. What does the engine return?', '["2.180000, the stated base price with nothing added, since the adder applies to gas distributors", "2.680000, the stated base price plus the s.167(6) adder", "2.630000, the commercial price that the base price reported for 2025 gives, a stated figure of the year before", "2.980000"]'::jsonb, 1, 's.167(6) sets the commercial sector price at the domestic base price plus US$0.50 per MMBtu, so on the stated 2.180000 the engine returns 2.680000; the course quotes both as the figures reported for 2026 and keys only the engine''s arithmetic on the stated input. Returning the base price unchanged is the power sector rule. 2.630000 is what the base price reported for 2025 gives. 2.980000 is the delivered power price of a case that states a transport tariff.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 4, 'A gas distributor states a negotiated price of 2.9 on a call that states price control applies and the domestic base price reported for 2026. What does the engine return?', '["The stated 2.900000 as the price, with a reason flagging that it breaks the ceiling of s.167(7)", "Refused by name at negotiatedPrice, since a negotiated price above the commercial price is not an input", "A price of 2.900000 with no reason, because a distributor negotiates its price freely under the Act", "A price of 2.680000 held at the ceiling of s.167(7), with the 2.900000 returned as the stated figure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'While price control applies, s.167(7) says a distributor''s price "shall not exceed" the commercial sector price, so the engine holds the price there: its reason reads "the negotiated price 2.9 exceeds the commercial sector price 2.68, so the price is held at 2.68 (s.167(7))", and it returns the stated figure as statedPrice with withinCeiling false and heldAt ceiling. The 2.68 is the commercial price on the stated base reported for 2026. The stated figure is no lawful price under s.167(7), so the engine does not return it as the price, and it refuses nothing: the call is valid and the breach is readable beside the price. A distributor negotiates only inside the ceiling while price control applies.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 5, 'A gas distributor''s negotiated price is exactly equal to the commercial sector price that the stated 2026 reported base price gives. How does the engine read it?', '["Above the ceiling, since the Act''s words \"shall not exceed\" make the ceiling itself unavailable to the distributor", "Refused, since a negotiated price must sit strictly below the commercial sector price in every stated call", "Held at the power sector price", "Within the ceiling"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ceiling is inclusive, and on the stated base price reported for 2026 the engine''s reason reads "the negotiated price 2.68 is at or below the commercial sector price 2.68, the ceiling for gas distributors (s.167(7))". "Shall not exceed" allows equality, so the ceiling itself is available. The engine refuses nothing here. The power sector price plays no part in a distributor''s price.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 6, 'A gas based industries call for urea states a CMPP of 450 US$ per tonne. The Fourth Schedule table holds NRP 1 and PRP 250. What price does the engine return?', '["1.800000, since EPF is 0.800000", "0.900000, the floor, since the formula result is held at the floor price for every gas based industry", "1.000000", "2.400000, adding PRP"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Fourth Schedule gives EPF = (CMPP - PRP) / PRP = (450 - 250) / 250 = 0.800000 and CP = NRP x (1 + EPF) = 1.800000, which lies inside the floor of 0.900000 and the stated base price, so it stands. The floor binds only when the formula falls below it. 1.000000 is the price when CMPP equals PRP. EPF subtracts PRP; adding it misreads the Schedule.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 7, 'The same urea call is run with a CMPP of 200 US$ per tonne. What does the engine return, and why?', '["0.800000, since the Fourth Schedule formula is applied as printed and the Act sets no lower bound for these industries", "A refusal, since a CMPP below the product reference price makes EPF negative and the formula undefined", "0.900000, the formula''s 0.8 being held at the s.168(2) floor", "1.000000, the National Reference Price, which the engine applies whenever EPF falls below zero for a product"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EPF is (200 - 250) / 250 = -0.200000, the formula gives 0.800000, and the engine''s reason reads "the formula gives 0.8, below the floor US$0.90 per MMBtu, so the price is held at 0.9 (s.168(2))". s.168(2) sets that floor, so the formula figure does not stand. A negative EPF is a result, and the engine refuses nothing. The NRP is one term of the formula and is no fallback price.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 8, 'Urea at a month-end product price of 600 US$ per tonne pushes the Fourth Schedule formula to 2.4, above the base price the call states (the figure reported for 2026). Where does the engine set the price?', '["It holds the price at the domestic base price stated for the call, the ceiling of s.168(3)", "It returns 2.400000, the formula figure", "It holds the price at the floor", "It refuses the call as out of range"]'::jsonb, 0, 's.168(3) makes the domestic base price of the year the ceiling for the gas based industries, and the engine''s reason reads "the formula gives 2.4, above the domestic base price 2.18, so the price is held at 2.18 (s.168(3))"; the ceiling figure here is the stated input reported for 2026, which the course grades nowhere. The formula figure does not stand above the ceiling. The floor acts only below 0.900000. A formula above the ceiling is a held result, and the engine refuses nothing here.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 9, 'For low sulphur diesel made by gas-to-liquids, the Fourth Schedule reads NRP 1 and PRP 325. With the month-end product price at 520 US$ per tonne, which gas price comes out?', '["The urea reference price of 250 applied to diesel, which the engine reads for every product in the table", "1.800000, the urea price at a CMPP of 450", "0.900000, the s.168(2) floor", "1.600000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'EPF is (520 - 325) / 325 = 0.600000 and CP is 1 x 1.600000, which lies inside the floor and the stated base price reported for 2026; the engine''s reason reads "the formula gives 1.6, inside the floor 0.9 and the domestic base price 2.18". Each product has its own PRP in the table, 325 for GTL diesel. 1.800000 is the urea result on other inputs. The floor does not bind at 1.600000.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 10, 'Suppose a caller sets the domestic base price at 0.8 US$ per MMBtu and asks for a gas based industries price. How does the engine respond?', '["A price of 0.800000, the base price acting as the ceiling below the floor", "A price of 0.900000", "A refusal: the base price must be at or above the US$0.90 floor", "A refusal because 0.8 differs from the base price reported for 2026, the only figure the engine will accept"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s message reads "domesticBasePrice must be at or above the gas based industries floor US$0.90 per MMBtu (s.168(2)) for a gas based industry price, which is capped at the domestic base price (s.168(3)); got 0.8": a ceiling below the floor leaves no price both rules allow. The engine does not choose one rule over the other. The base price is a stated input, and any figure at or above the floor is accepted.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 11, 'Someone pricing the power sector under price control also fills in negotiatedPrice with 2. Quote the engine''s response.', '["A power price of 2.000000, since a negotiated figure replaces the domestic base price for any sector", "negotiatedPrice must be given only for sector ''gas-distributor'' while priceControlApplies is true; got 2", "negotiatedPrice must be stated for a gas distributor", "A power price with the negotiated figure ignored"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine refuses the input by name: "negotiatedPrice must be given only for sector ''gas-distributor'' while priceControlApplies is true; got 2". Under price control the power sector price is the domestic base price under s.167(5), so no negotiated figure replaces it. The message about a figure that must be stated is the refusal of a distributor call with no negotiated price. The engine never ignores an input it was given.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 12, 'A power sector call states the base price reported for 2026 and a transport tariff, and the engine returns a price of 2.180000 and a delivered price of 2.980000. What does the engine''s basis say about the two figures?', '["The tariff is folded into the power price, since s.167(5) prices the power sector at the power plant''s own gate", "The delivered price replaces the price, since a transport tariff turns every sector price into a delivered one", "The tariff is deducted from the seller''s side, since the Act puts the transport cost on the producing lessee", "The price is at the delivery point; the stated tariff is added for the delivered price"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s basis reads "prices at the marketable natural gas delivery point; the stated transport tariff is added for the delivered price (s.167(8), s.168(4))". s.167(8) puts the transport cost from the delivery point to the buyers'' facilities on the buyers, so it is added on top and the delivery point price stands. Both figures rest on the stated base price reported for 2026. Folding the tariff in or deducting it from the seller misreads s.167(8).'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 13, 'A caller states NRP 1.2 and PRP 300 as an illustrative regulation (synthetic) for ammonia, with a CMPP of 450. The engine''s reason prints the formula''s result as the double it holds, a run of nines just under the round figure. Which figure does the course quote for the price?', '["The run of nines in the reason, since the reason carries the double the engine holds and is the more exact figure", "1.800000, the numeric field at six decimals", "1.500000, EPF with no NRP applied", "The Schedule''s own NRP 1 and PRP 250"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course quotes the numeric field at six decimals, 1.800000, and quotes a reason only verbatim as the engine''s words, where a figure is the shortest round-trip decimal of the double. EPF is (450 - 300) / 300 = 0.500000, and CP is 1.2 x 1.500000, so a figure without the NRP drops a term. The Fourth Schedule lets the Authority change NRP and PRP by regulation, and the engine uses the stated values with their source.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 14, 'Where does the course''s reported figure of 2.180000 US$ per MMBtu for the 2026 domestic base price come from, and how may it be used?', '["It is reported by BusinessDay (31 March 2026) and by Advocaat Law Practice through Legal 500 (7 April 2026), the regulator''s circular having not been read, and is used only as a stated input", "It is read from the regulator''s circular effective 1 April 2026, which the engine holds as its default", "It is printed in the Act''s Third Schedule", "It is a graded figure that the capstone asks for"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s own basis says the figures "are reported by BusinessDay (31 March 2026) and by Advocaat Law Practice through Legal 500 (7 April 2026); the regulator''s circular was not read", and it holds no default. The Third Schedule prints principles for the price and no figure. The course grades no domestic base price, and no capstone uses one.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-domestic-gas-prices', 15, 'The Third Schedule of the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 sets principles for the domestic base price. What does its paragraph 1(a) require of the price?', '["A level no higher than the Fourth Schedule''s National Reference Price", "A fixed US$ figure printed in the Schedule, re-read by the Authority each year and adjusted for inflation", "A level that brings forward enough gas for the domestic market voluntarily", "The lowest supply price on the national supply curve, which the Commission identifies for the strategic sectors"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Paragraph 1(a) reads "the price must be of a level to bring forward sufficient natural gas supplies for the domestic market on a voluntary basis by the upstream petroleum industry". The National Reference Price is a Fourth Schedule term for the gas based industries formula. The Schedule prints principles and no figure. The supply curve is a tool of the Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation Regulations 2022 (r.5(2)) for the supply price, a different instrument.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 1, 'Under the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 s.110(1)(a), what must the Commission do before 1 March of each year?', '["Determine the domestic base price under the Third Schedule", "Fix the penalty rate", "Approve every export supply project that lessees have put forward for the following contract year", "Prescribe the domestic gas delivery obligation and allocate it among all lessees"]'::jsonb, 3, 's.110(1)(a) reads "prescribe and allocate the domestic gas delivery obligation among all lessees before 1st March of each year based on the domestic gas demand requirements". The domestic base price is the Authority''s under s.167(1). The penalty rate is set in s.110(8) and may be adjusted by regulation under s.110(9), which is no annual step. Export approvals follow prior compliance under s.110(15); they are no part of the allocation.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 2, 'A golden lessee holds voluntary contracts of 1000.000000 with the strategic sectors against an obligation of 1000.000000, and delivers 200.000000. What does the engine return?', '["Deemed fulfilled under s.110(2)(a), with no penalty", "800.000000 penalised at 3.500000, a penalty of 2800.000000, since deemed fulfilment needs contracts above the obligation", "800.000000 undelivered and excused", "A refusal, since delivered gas below the obligation cannot be read together with voluntary contracts that meet it"]'::jsonb, 0, 's.110(2) deems fulfilment where "the volume of the contracts is equal to or higher than the domestic gas delivery obligation", so equality is enough, and the engine''s reason reads "voluntary contracts of 1000 are at or above the obligation 1000: the lessee is deemed to have fulfilled its obligation (s.110(2)(a))". 2800.000000 is what the engine returns when the contracts fall one short, at 999.000000. Nothing is excused here, since no excuse is stated. The engine accepts both inputs together.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 3, 'One unit short: voluntary strategic-sector contracts of 999.000000 face an allocation of 1000.000000, and only 200.000000 reaches the buyers, with nothing excused. What penalty is due?', '["0.000000, since contracts within one unit of the obligation are close enough for the lessee to be deemed to have fulfilled it", "3.500000, on the single unit by which the contracts fall short of the obligation that the Commission allocated", "2800.000000, on 800.000000 at 3.500000", "2800.000000 less the value of the 999.000000 contracted, since contracted gas counts toward the penalty"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Deemed fulfilment needs contracts equal to or higher than the obligation, and 999.000000 is one short, so the engine measures what was delivered: 1000.000000 less 200.000000 leaves 800.000000 undelivered, penalised at 3.500000 for 2800.000000. The Act gives no margin below the obligation. The penalty falls on gas not delivered, and the gap in the contracts is no measure of it. Contracts that do not reach the obligation reduce nothing.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 4, 'The Ekene power plant''s synthetic 2028 allocation sets an obligation of 6825000.000000 MMBtu, and 5460000.000000 is delivered. What undelivered quantity does the engine return before any excuse?', '["688800.000000", "1365000.000000, the obligation less the gas delivered", "676200.000000", "6825000.000000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Undelivered is the obligation less the gas delivered when that is positive, 6825000.000000 less 5460000.000000, and the engine''s reason reads "delivered 5460000 against the obligation 6825000 leaves 1365000 undelivered". 688800.000000 is the part excused because the purchaser could not accept. 676200.000000 is what is left to penalise after that excuse. The whole obligation is not undelivered, since most of it was delivered.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 5, 'In the power plant''s 2028 allocation, 688800.000000 of the 1365000.000000 undelivered is excused because the purchaser could not accept the allocated volumes. What penalty does the engine return?', '["0.000000, since a purchaser''s outage excuses the lessee for the whole of the year and every quantity left undelivered", "The whole 1365000.000000 at 3.500000, since an excuse under s.110(10) reduces the export restriction and leaves the penalty", "2366700.000000, on 676200.000000 at 3.500000", "676200.000000 at the take-or-pay price of the power plant agreement, since the obligation is settled in contract money"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The excuse under s.110(10)(b) covers only the quantity stated, 688800.000000, so 676200.000000 remains and is penalised at 3.500000 US$ per MMBtu, which is 2366700.000000, as the engine''s reason prints. The excuse does not reach past its own quantity. It reduces the penalised quantity itself. The rate is the Act''s US$3.50 of s.110(8) and the Regulations'' r.6(1), and a take-or-pay price plays no part.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 6, 'A golden lessee leaves 500.000000 undelivered and states four excuses: purchaser non-payment 100.000000, force majeure 150.000000, purchaser cannot accept 50.000000 and transport unavailable 25.000000. What does the engine penalise?', '["500.000000, since excuses stated out of the Act''s order are refused one by one and none is applied", "400.000000, since only the first excuse listed in the call applies", "0.000000, since four excuses together clear any quantity", "175.000000, a penalty of 612.500000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine applies the excuses in the order of s.110(10), (a) force majeure 150.000000, (b) 50.000000, (c) 25.000000 and (d) 100.000000, whatever order the call lists them in, 325.000000 in all, and penalises the 175.000000 left at 3.500000 for 612.500000. The order in the call changes nothing. Every stated excuse applies up to the undelivered quantity left. Four excuses that sum to less than the undelivered quantity leave a remainder.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 7, 'A golden lessee leaves 400.000000 undelivered and states force majeure of 250.000000 and transport unavailable of 300.000000. How much of the transport excuse does the engine apply?', '["150.000000, up to the undelivered quantity left", "300.000000, the stated quantity, so that 550.000000 is excused in all", "0.000000, since force majeure alone covers the year", "200.000000, half of the undelivered quantity, split evenly between the two excuses because both of them are stated for the year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine applies force majeure first, s.110(10)(a), 250.000000, and then the transport excuse, s.110(10)(c), only up to the 150.000000 still undelivered; its reason reads "150 is excused: the allocated gas could not be transported for reasons beyond the lessee''s control (s.110(10)(c))", and then "the whole undelivered quantity is excused; no penalty". No excuse is applied beyond the undelivered quantity. Force majeure covers only its stated 250.000000. The Act gives no rule for splitting.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 8, 'A lessee has signed a gas purchase and sale agreement with a wholesale supplier of the strategic sectors that states a penalty of 5 per MMBtu, and leaves 400.000000 undelivered. What penalty does the engine return?', '["1400.000000 at US$3.50, since the Act''s rate replaces any rate a signed agreement states for the same failure", "2000.000000, at the agreement''s rate of 5", "Both rates added together and charged on the undelivered 400.000000, the Act''s and the agreement''s", "1700.000000 at 4.25"]'::jsonb, 1, 's.110(8) makes the penalty under such an agreement "as stated in that agreement", and r.6(2) of the Regulations sets it at "not less than" US$3.50, so a stated 5 applies: 400 x 5 is 2000.000000, and the engine''s basis reads "the agreement''s rate 5 per MMBtu (s.110(8) proviso), at or above the US$3.50 minimum of r.6(2)". The Act''s own rate is the floor for an agreement. The two rates are not added. 4.25 is an illustrative adjusted rate from a different case.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 9, 'Suppose the wholesale agreement names only 2 US$ per MMBtu as its penalty for failing to deliver, with 400.000000 short. Which figure does the engine charge?', '["800.000000, at the agreement''s rate of 2", "A refusal: the rate is below 3.5", "2000.000000", "1400.000000, the rate lifted to the US$3.50 minimum of r.6(2)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Regulations'' r.6(2) says the penalty under a signed agreement "shall not be less than" the r.6(1) amount, so the engine''s basis reads "the agreement''s rate 2 per MMBtu is below the US$3.50 minimum of r.6(2), so 3.5 applies", and 400 x 3.5 is 1400.000000. Applying 2 would put the agreement below the stated minimum. The engine applies the minimum; it does not refuse. 2000.000000 is the penalty at an agreement rate of 5.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 10, 'A call states both an agreementPenaltyRate and a penaltyRate. What does the engine return?', '["The higher of the two rates", "The agreement''s rate, with the other ignored", "A refusal: penaltyRate must be left out when an agreement rate is stated", "Two penalties, one at each rate, so that the lessee sees what the undelivered gas costs under each basis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s message reads "penaltyRate must be left out when agreementPenaltyRate is stated (state one rate basis); got {"value":4,"source":"x"}". The rate basis is one stated choice, so the engine does not pick between two, does not drop one silently and does not return two penalties.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 11, 'A call states a penalty rate of 4.25 per MMBtu with its source, an illustrative Commission regulation (synthetic), and 400.000000 is undelivered. What does the engine return, and under which provision?', '["1700.000000, at the adjusted rate the Commission may prescribe under s.110(9)", "1400.000000, since the engine applies US$3.50 whatever rate a call states", "A refusal: the rate must be 3.5", "2000.000000, since an adjusted rate is rounded up to the nearest whole US$ per MMBtu before it is applied to the gas"]'::jsonb, 0, 's.110(9) reads "The penalty amount of US $3.50 per MMBtu referred to under subsection (8) may be adjusted as the Commission may prescribe in a regulation made under this Act", and the engine applies a stated rate with its source: 400 x 4.25 is 1700.000000. It does not hold to US$3.50 when a stated adjusted rate is given, and it neither refuses nor rounds the rate.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 12, 'When the engine penalises a quantity above zero, what does it report about export supply, and what does it do with that finding?', '["It adds a second penalty for the export restriction at US$3.50 per MMBtu on the quantity penalised", "It reports the s.110(14)(a) and s.110(15) restriction and gives it no price", "It reduces the next year''s obligation by the penalised quantity, so the export restriction lapses the year after", "Nothing about export"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s reason ends "the lessee may not supply new midstream gas export operations (s.110(14)(a)) and export supply approvals require prior compliance (s.110(15))", and it reports the restriction as a flag with no money attached. No second penalty is charged. The obligation is a stated input each year, and the engine does not carry a quantity into the next one. The flag is always reported when a quantity is penalised.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 13, 'Regulation 6(3)(b) of the Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation Regulations 2022 deems the obligation met if the Commission fails to investigate a claimed excuse within 90 days. How does the engine treat this rule?', '["It counts 90 days from the end of the contract year and deems the obligation met if no investigation date is given", "It treats every stated excuse as uninvestigated", "It refuses any excuse that is stated without an investigation date, since the date decides whether the obligation is deemed met", "It does not compute it; excuses apply as stated, and the rule is taught as a concept"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s basis states that "the 90-day investigation rule of r.6(3) and the compensation to customer-clients of s.110(13) are not computed": excuses apply as stated, and the rule is a concept in this course. The engine takes no investigation date, so it can neither count days nor refuse for a missing one, and it deems nothing met on the rule''s account.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 14, 'Under the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 s.110(16), how are domestic gas delivery contracts signed before the Act''s effective date treated?', '["They count toward the lessee''s obligation", "They are void under the Act", "They are excused from it", "They count toward the obligation only for their first contract year after the Act took effect, and lapse from then on"]'::jsonb, 0, 's.110(16) reads "Domestic gas delivery contracts entered into by lessees or licensees prior to the effective date and continuing after the effective date, shall be counted towards their domestic gas delivery obligation". The Act does not void them, does not exclude their gas, and sets no one-year limit.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-domestic-gas-delivery-obligation', 15, 'A call lists an excuse under the key pipelineOutage. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["The quantity excused as force majeure", "The quantity excused under s.110(10)(c), since a pipeline outage is gas that cannot be transported", "excused.pipelineOutage is not an accepted key; the accepted keys of excused are forceMajeure, purchaserCannotAccept, transportUnavailable, purchaserNonPayment", "No excuse applied and the whole quantity penalised, since the engine drops an excuse it cannot read"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses the unknown key and lists the four excuses it reads, one for each of s.110(10)(a) to (d). A pipeline outage may well be a transport excuse in fact, but the call must state it under transportUnavailable; the engine never maps a key on a guess. It does not drop the excuse silently either, since the penalty would move.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'In 2030 the Ekene export feed buyer (synthetic) declares force majeure of 1260000.000000 against an ACQ of 22995000.000000, with take-or-pay at 90 percent. What take-or-pay quantity does the engine return?', '["20695500.000000, which is 90 percent of the full ACQ, since force majeure declared by the buyer reduces nothing", "21385000.000000, the gas taken that year", "19561500.000000, on the reduced Adjusted ACQ", "22995000.000000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Force majeure comes off the ACQ whichever party declares it, so the Adjusted ACQ falls by 1260000.000000 and 90 percent of it is 19561500.000000, as the export ledger prints. 20695500.000000 is the take-or-pay quantity of a 365 day year with nothing reduced. 21385000.000000 is the year''s take, measured against the take-or-pay quantity. 22995000.000000 is the ACQ itself.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'With the export feed''s make-up order ''after-top-quantity'', 3219250.000000 of make-up open and a 2030 take of 21385000.000000 against a take-or-pay quantity of 19561500.000000, how much make-up does the engine take?', '["1823500.000000", "3219250.000000, the whole open aggregate, since the take clears the take-or-pay quantity with room to spare", "0.000000, since a force majeure year gives the buyer no make-up", "576600.000000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ''after-top-quantity'' make-up is taken only after the year''s take-or-pay quantity is taken, so the draw is 21385000.000000 less 19561500.000000, 1823500.000000, and the engine''s reason names the order in its words, "make-up only after the take-or-pay quantity of the year is taken". The take above the threshold limits the draw, so the whole aggregate is not released. A year with force majeure still counts its take. 576600.000000 is the make-up drawn in 2033, from the 2032 entry.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'ESMAP Report 152/93 (January 1993) para 6.59 reads: "Normally, make-up quantities are accounted for after the minimum-pay quantity for the year has been taken." Which of the engine''s stated recovery orders computes that description?', '["''after-adjusted-acq'', since the Adjusted ACQ is the minimum-pay quantity of the ESMAP report under a different name", "''first'', since ESMAP describes the normal arrangement and the engine''s first order is its normal one", "None of them, since the engine computes only the model agreement''s order and refuses the orders of the older guides", "''after-top-quantity''"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s basis names ''after-adjusted-acq'' as the reference text''s order (the Commonwealth model agreement''s Article 12.7.1) and ''after-top-quantity'' and ''first'' as variants it also computes; make-up counted after the year''s minimum-pay (take-or-pay) quantity is ''after-top-quantity''. The Adjusted ACQ is the base of the take-or-pay quantity, which is a percentage of it. ''first'' takes make-up in priority, before the year''s own quantity. The engine computes all three orders and takes whichever the contract states.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'HMRC''s Oil Taxation Manual OT05435 (updated 19 December 2019) says make-up may be taken "in priority over that period''s contract amount; or only when the minimum for that period has been taken." Which engine order is the first of those two?', '["''after-adjusted-acq'', since it is the order the reference text names and every guide describes it first", "''first'', make-up taken in priority", "''after-top-quantity''", "''fifo'', the first in first out order, which the makeUp.order input accepts beside the other three"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Make-up in priority over the period''s contract amount is the engine''s ''first'' order, whose reason reads "make-up taken in priority, before the year''s own quantity"; HMRC''s second arrangement is ''after-top-quantity''. ''after-adjusted-acq'' is the model agreement''s order. First in first out is how entries are drawn under every order, and "fifo" is refused as an order: "makeUp.order must be one of \"after-adjusted-acq\", \"after-top-quantity\", \"first\"; got \"fifo\"".'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'A golden two-year case pays for a deficiency of 800.000000 in 2027 after a zero take. In 2028, the last contract year, the buyer takes 1000.000000 against an Adjusted ACQ of 1000.000000 under ''after-adjusted-acq'' with ''forfeit''. What does the engine return for the 800.000000?', '["800.000000 taken as make-up", "200.000000 taken, 600.000000 forfeited", "A refund of 800.000000 at 3", "Nothing drawn; the buyer loses all 800.000000 at the close"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine''s reasons read "make-up aggregate 800 available and none taken, because taken 1000 does not exceed the Adjusted ACQ 1000" and "the delivery period ends with make-up of 800 unrecovered; the buyer forfeits it". Under this order the threshold is the Adjusted ACQ, and a take equal to it leaves nothing above. Measuring above the take-or-pay quantity is the other order. The case states forfeit, so nothing is refunded.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'In 2031 the export feed takes 23915000.000000, draws 1395750.000000 of make-up and has a take-or-pay quantity of 20695500.000000, with carry-forward stated above the take-or-pay quantity. What surplus does the engine carry forward?', '["1823750.000000, what is counted above the take-or-pay quantity", "The whole take above the take-or-pay quantity", "1395750.000000, the make-up drawn", "None, since a year that takes make-up carries nothing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Make-up gas counts toward the deficiency year it was paid in, so 2031 counts 23915000.000000 less 1395750.000000, 22519250.000000, and the surplus is the part above 20695500.000000, which is 1823750.000000; the engine''s reason reads "22519250 counted exceeds the take-or-pay quantity 20695500 by 1823750, carried forward to the end of 2034". Taking the whole take above the threshold counts the make-up twice. The make-up drawn is no surplus. A year may draw make-up and still carry a surplus.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'The power plant ledger''s totals rest on the fixture''s take-or-pay and contract price of 2.180000, a stated planning assumption: regular revenue 120529584.000000, deficiency payments 2279844.000000 and seller shortfall damages 7875.000000, with no make-up revenue and no refund. What net to the seller does the engine return?', '["120529584.000000, the regular revenue alone, since deficiency payments are held until the make-up is taken", "1,883,223,633.172000", "122801553.000000", "The regular revenue less the damages, with the deficiency payments left out because they are no revenue"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine builds the net as the gas counted at the contract price plus make-up revenue plus deficiency payments less damages and any refund: 120529584.000000 plus 2279844.000000 less 7875.000000 is 122801553.000000, on the stated planning price. Deficiency payments are paid in the deficiency year and are part of the net. 1,883,223,633.172000 is the export feed''s net over its term. Leaving out the payments ignores what the buyer paid for gas it did not take.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'Priced at 4, a 2027 deficiency of 200.000000 opens make-up usable for two years, but the agreement ends with 2028, when 150.000000 is recovered. With ''forfeit'' stated, what becomes of the balance?', '["50.000000 refunded at 4", "50.000000 forfeited when the delivery period ends", "50.000000 kept open until the end of 2029, the last year of the entry''s make-up period after the deficiency year", "Nothing, the payment covering it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s reasons read "make-up of 150 taken from the make-up aggregate 200" and "the delivery period ends with make-up of 50 unrecovered; the buyer forfeits it". The case states forfeit, so no refund. The term ends in 2028, before the period would, so the end-of-term rule settles the rest. The payment of 200 x 4 = 800 bought the right to 200.000000 of gas; 50.000000 of it goes untaken.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'In the golden case with make-up of 100.000000 from 2027 and 50.000000 from 2028, each for 3 contract years, the 2027 entry is fully drawn by 2030 under the order ''after-adjusted-acq''. In 2031 the buyer takes nothing above the Adjusted ACQ. What does the engine report for 2031?', '["40.000000 from 2027 expired", "40.000000 kept open to 2032", "70.000000 expired, the whole aggregate", "40.000000 from 2028 expired unrecovered at the end of 2031"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 2028 entry runs to the end of 2031 and 40.000000 of it is left after the 2030 draw of 10.000000, so the engine''s reason reads "make-up of 40 from 2028 expired unrecovered at the end of 2031, the last year of its make-up period". The 2027 entry has nothing left by then. 2031 is the last year of the 2028 entry, so nothing stays open. 70.000000 was the aggregate after 2029; the 2030 draw cut it to 40.000000.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'The export feed''s term ends in 2036 with 457950.000000 of make-up unrecovered from 2035, and the fixture states a refund. At which price does the engine refund it?', '["7.898300, the 2035 take-or-pay price of the deficiency year, since the buyer is repaid what it paid for that gas", "8.950200, the price of December 2036, the last month of the term, since the refund is made when the term ends", "8.081300, the 2036 take-or-pay price", "0.808130"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The model agreement''s Alternative 2 refunds at the take-or-pay price of the contract year in which the delivery period expires, and the engine''s reason reads "the seller refunds 457950 x 8.0813", the 2036 annual average the fixture states as its take-or-pay price. 7.898300 is the 2035 price. 8.950200 is the last month''s price, which is Alternative 2 of the take-or-pay price rule and is not the fixture''s basis. 0.808130 is the 2036 make-up price.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'The plant''s 2033 take falls 357000.000000 below its take-or-pay quantity. At the held 2.180000 (the fixture''s stated planning assumption), what does the buyer pay for it?', '["778260.000000, on that planning price", "1501584.000000", "0.000000, the 2033 deficiency being covered by the make-up still open from 2028", "252000.000000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s reason reads "the deficiency payment is 357000 x 2.18 = 778260", on the fixture''s held planning price. 1501584.000000 is the 2028 payment on 688800.000000. The 2028 entry expired at the end of 2031, and make-up is gas already paid for, so it never pays a later deficiency. 252000.000000 is a quantity, the part of the 2033 entry forfeited in 2034.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'A golden case with a make-up period of 5 contract years ends in 2029 with 150.000000 of make-up open from 2027, under ''forfeit''. What does the buyer receive for it?', '["600.000000, the 150.000000 refunded at the last year''s price of 4", "Nothing: the buyer forfeits the 150.000000", "The 150.000000 as gas in a year after the term, since the make-up period of 5 contract years runs to the end of 2032", "500.000000, the 2027 payment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s reason reads "the delivery period ends with make-up of 150 unrecovered; the buyer forfeits it". 600.000000 is what the same ledger returns under ''refund''. No year exists after the term, so the period cannot be used past it. 500.000000 was the 2027 payment on 200.000000, of which 50.000000 was taken in 2028.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'The Commonwealth Secretariat''s model agreement (2025, CC BY 4.0) defines the Make-Up Period. When does it run?', '["From the deficiency year itself", "Until the end of the Delivery Period", "In calendar years after payment", "In the Contract Years immediately following the deficiency year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The definition reads "Contract Years immediately following the Contract Year corresponding to such Buyer''s Annual Deficiency Quantity, within which Buyer has a right to make up", which is why the engine''s period for a deficiency year y runs from y + 1 to y + N. The deficiency year is not inside it. The period is a stated number of years and does not run to the end of the term. It is counted in contract years from the deficiency.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'Under the Commonwealth model agreement''s Article 12.7, what happens to a quantity the buyer does not make up within its make-up period?', '["The seller refunds it at the take-or-pay price of the year the period ends in, under the model''s main text", "It rolls into the carry-forward aggregate", "The right terminates and the buyer forfeits it", "The period is extended by one contract year each time the buyer is unable to take the gas above the Adjusted ACQ"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Article 12.7 reads "the right to make up such equivalent quantity shall terminate, and Buyer shall forfeit any and all rights with respect to such Buyer''s Annual Deficiency Quantity." A refund is the model''s Alternative 2 for make-up still open at the end of the Delivery Period, a different moment. Carry-forward is built from excess takes. The model prints no rolling extension of a make-up period.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'Three years after the train outage the export buyer under-takes again: 19599000.000000 counted in 2032 against 20752200.000000 due, with 1823750.000000 of carry-forward standing ready and a cap of half a deficiency. How much of it is used?', '["1153200.000000, the whole deficiency, since the carry-forward available is larger than the deficiency", "576600.000000, from 2031", "1823750.000000", "576600.000000 from 2028, the oldest surplus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The deficiency is 1153200.000000 and the cap allows half of it, 576600.000000, drawn first in first out from the oldest surplus still open, which is 2031''s; the engine''s reason reads "a carry-forward credit of 576600 (at most 50% of the deficiency, first in first out: 576600 from 2031) leaves 576600". The cap binds even when more is available. The 2028 surplus expired at the end of 2031.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'For 2035 the export feed''s take-or-pay price is 7.898300. The year''s deficiency of 2299500.000000 draws a credit of 1149750.000000. What deficiency payment does the engine return?', '["The whole deficiency priced at 7.898300, since the credit reduces only the make-up that opens", "4472628.540000, the payment for 2032, the other year of the second half of the term in which the buyer fell short", "31575852.662500, the payment for 2029", "9081070.425000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine prices the deficiency left after the credit: 1149750 x 7.8983, which the course quotes as the field 9081070.425000. The credit comes off the deficiency before it is priced. 4472628.540000 and 31575852.662500 are the payments for 2032 and 2029.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'Which carry-forward does the engine report as expiring unused in 2034 on the export feed ledger, where carry-forward is stated for 3 contract years?', '["1247150.000000, the rest of 2031''s surplus", "2182900.000000 from 2033, since the newest surplus lapses first", "694050.000000 from 2028", "2069500.000000 from 2034, the surplus of the year itself, which cannot be carried forward into the year after"]'::jsonb, 0, '2031''s surplus of 1823750.000000 lost 576600.000000 to the 2032 credit, and the remaining 1247150.000000 reaches the end of its period at the end of 2034; the engine''s reason reads "carry-forward of 1247150 from 2031 expired unused at the end of 2034". 2033''s surplus runs to 2036. 694050.000000 from 2028 expired in 2031. 2034''s own surplus is carried to the end of 2037.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'Two surpluses above the Adjusted ACQ, 100.000000 (2027) and 50.000000 (2028), may each be credited for 2 contract years at a cap of 100 percent. The 2027 one is used up in 2029. What happens when 2030 falls 200.000000 short, priced at 3?', '["Credits of 150.000000 in all", "200.000000, all of it", "50.000000 credited, 450.000000 paid", "No credit and a payment of 600.000000, since a surplus above the Adjusted ACQ may only be used within the year after"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only the 2028 surplus of 50.000000 is left, and its period runs to the end of 2030, so the credit is 50.000000 and the payment is 150 x 3 = 450; the engine''s reason reads "a carry-forward credit of 50 (at most 100% of the deficiency, first in first out: 50 from 2028) leaves 150". The 2027 surplus was used up in 2029. A credit cannot exceed the surplus open. A surplus lasts its stated period of 2 contract years.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'ESMAP Report 152/93 (January 1993) para 6.61 says "gas taken in excess of the minimum pay could be credited against the minimum-pay quantities in later years". Which right, in this course''s vocabulary, does that describe?', '["Make-up", "Carry-forward", "Seller shortfall damages", "The Adjusted ACQ reduction for a permitted reduction, which lowers the minimum-pay quantity of the years that follow"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course legislates carry-forward as takes above the stated base credited against a later deficiency, capped, for a stated period, which is what para 6.61 describes. Make-up runs the other way: gas paid for in a deficiency year and taken later. Seller shortfall damages price gas the seller did not make available. A permitted reduction lowers the Adjusted ACQ of its own year.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'The export feed fixture prices make-up gas at 10 percent of the contract price. Over the term the buyer takes 4487650.000000 of make-up. What make-up revenue does the engine return?', '["3700831.335000, the refund paid at the end of the term for the make-up the buyer did not take", "45129551.627500, the deficiency payments of the term, since make-up revenue is the payment for make-up gas", "4487650.000000 MMBtu of make-up", "4242052.824500"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine prices each year''s make-up at that year''s stated make-up price and sums it to 4242052.824500 over the term. 3700831.335000 is the refund, money running back to the buyer. 45129551.627500 is the deficiency payments, paid when each deficiency arose. 4487650.000000 is a quantity in MMBtu, the make-up taken.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'On a golden day with a DCQ of 100.000000, a delivery tolerance of 5.000000 and a properly nominated quantity of 100.000000, the seller makes 94.000000 available and the buyer takes all of it. What seller shortfall does the engine return?', '["1.000000", "The whole gap below the nomination, tolerance ignored", "0.000000, since the tolerance covers the day", "5.000000, the tolerance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The seller shortfall is the properly nominated quantity less the tolerance less the quantity made available, (100 - 5) - 94, and the engine''s reason reads "the seller made 94 available against a properly nominated 100 less the tolerance 5: seller shortfall 1". Ignoring the tolerance charges the seller for the whole gap. The tolerance covers 5, and one unit more is the seller''s. 5.000000 is the tolerance itself, which the seller owes nothing on (and the buyer shortfall of that day).'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'No lag, a three-month mean and monthly repricing: with oil at 60, 62 and 64 over 2025-01 to 2025-03, what does 1 + 0.1 x oil give for 2025-03?', '["7.400000, the 2025-03 index alone", "7.000000, the average of the three months before 2025-03", "7.200000, on an average of 62.000000", "The averaged index read straight as the price, since the constant and the slope apply to a single month"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With a lag of 0 the three-month window ends in the delivery month, 2025-01 to 2025-03, averaging 62.000000, so the price is 1 + 0.1 x 62 = 7.200000. The single month is a window of 1. A window ending the month before is a lag of 1. The formula applies to the averaged index as to any other.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'Annual repricing: the line 1 + 0.1 x oil reads a six-month window closed one month early, but its price changes only once every 12 months, counting from 2025-07. Which price stands in 2026-06?', '["7.500000, the block''s first price, on 2025-01 to 2025-06", "9.366667", "8.900000, from a window ending in 2026-05", "A new price each quarter"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every month of a 12 month block carries the price of the block''s first month, so 2025-07 to 2026-06 all read the window 2025-01 to 2025-06, averaging 65.000000, and price 7.500000. 9.366667 is a quarterly reset case''s figure for 2026-06. A window ending in 2026-05 would need a new block. A quarterly reset is a different term.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'For April 2027 the export feed averages its oil index over 2026-10 to 2027-03. What average and price does the engine return?', '["76.925000, priced 9.731000, since April 2027 still sits in the January price block of the quarterly reset", "71.461667, priced 9.075400", "61.730000, the March 2027 index", "71.461667, priced 9.731000 because the price holds for six months"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Blocks of three months run from 2027-01, so April opens the second one, and its window is the half year closed a month earlier, 2026-10 to 2027-03: the oil mean is 71.461667 and the line 0.5 + 0.12 x oil gives 9.075400. The pair 76.925000 and 9.731000 belongs to January to March. A single March reading is a one-month window. Each block holds its price for three months only.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'An index of 11.234346 is passed straight through as the contract price, and the contract states the model agreement''s four-decimal rounding. Which price is invoiced?', '["The figure rounded one decimal at a time, the sixth first and then the fifth", "11.234346, since the rule reads the fifth decimal and 4 leaves the figure untouched", "11.234400, since the digits past the fourth decimal are rounded up whenever any of them is above zero", "11.234300"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The price is computed in full before the rule reads its fifth decimal: the fifth decimal of 11.234346 is 4, so the fourth decimal stays and the price is 11.234300. Rounding one decimal at a time is not the Article 15.4 rule. Leaving the figure untouched ignores the rounding to four decimals. Rounding up on any trailing digit is no rule the model prints.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'A band of 3.5 to 4.5 US$ per MMBtu is put on the straight line 0.8 + 0.1485 x jcc. What comes out when jcc sits at 22.5?', '["3.500000, since the line starts below the floor", "4.500000, the ceiling, which binds from the midpoint of the band upward", "4.141250, with no clamp", "4.000000, the midpoint of the band"]'::jsonb, 2, '0.8 + 0.1485 x 22.5 is 4.141250, inside the band of 3.5 to 4.5, so the engine returns it unclamped. The floor binds only in a month where the line is below it. The ceiling binds only above 4.5. No rule moves a price to the midpoint of its band.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'A golden index series lists 2025-01 and then 2025-03. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["months[1].month must be 2025-02, the month after 2025-01 (the series is consecutive); got \"2025-03\"", "A price for 2025-02 read by straight-line interpolation between the months on either side", "months must cover the averaging window", "A series priced with 2025-02 left out"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Index months must follow one another, so the engine stops at the second entry and says which month it wanted and which it got. Filling 2025-02 by interpolation would invent an index value that later prices depend on, and leaving it out would shift every window after it. The window message is a separate refusal, for a series that starts too late for the window a price needs.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'A golden basket prices 5 x the weighted sum of each index over its base, with weights of 0.6 for fo and 0.4 for cpi and base values of 400 and 100, on a 2 month window with a lag of 1. For 2025-12 the averages are fo 410.000000 and cpi 100.500000. What price does the engine return?', '["5.030000, the next month", "5.105000, with a floor", "5.000000, the price at the base values, since a basket reprices only when an index moves outside its band", "5.085000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine weights each averaged index over its base value, fo 410.000000 over 400 and cpi 100.500000 over 100, sums them at 0.6 and 0.4 and multiplies by 5, which gives 5.085000. 5.030000 is the 2026-01 price. 5.105000 is 2026-01 again with an index floor on fo. This case reprices every month on its averaged indices and states no band.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'Suppose a city distribution company agrees 2.5 US$ per MMBtu with its supplier, on the base price reported for 2026. Which figure does the engine price?', '["2.680000, lifted to the commercial price on the stated base, since a distributor pays the commercial sector price", "2.500000, the negotiated figure as it stands", "2.180000, the stated power price", "A refusal, since a distributor must state a price equal to the commercial price that the stated base gives"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Distributors negotiate under s.167(7), and the engine returns the negotiated 2.500000 with the reason "the negotiated price 2.5 is at or below the commercial sector price 2.68, the ceiling for gas distributors (s.167(7))", on a call that states price control applies, that ceiling being the commercial price on the stated base reported for 2026. The Act caps a distributor''s price and sets no floor at the ceiling, so nothing is lifted. The power sector price is a different sector''s rule. Any negotiated price is accepted and tested against the ceiling.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'A urea call states a CMPP equal to the product reference price, 250 US$ per tonne. What does the engine return?', '["0.900000, the floor", "0.000000", "1.000000, with EPF 0.000000", "2.000000, since EPF is (250 + 250) / 250"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EPF is (250 - 250) / 250 = 0.000000, so CP = 1 x (1 + 0) = 1.000000, inside the floor and the stated base price; the engine''s reason reads "the formula gives 1, inside the floor 0.9" and the stated base. The floor does not bind at 1.000000. A price of zero would drop the NRP. EPF subtracts the PRP.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'Suppose the month-end urea price collapses to 0 US$ per tonne. Which gas price does the engine set?', '["0.900000: a zero result is raised to the minimum", "0.000000, the formula price", "A refusal, since a product price of zero makes the Fourth Schedule formula meaningless", "1.000000, the NRP"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A product price of nothing drives EPF to -1.000000 and the Fourth Schedule result to 0.000000; the engine''s reason reads "the formula gives 0, below the floor US$0.90 per MMBtu, so the price is held at 0.9 (s.168(2))". A zero price for gas is below what s.168(2) allows. A CMPP of zero is a stated input, so nothing is refused. The NRP of 1 multiplies (1 + EPF) and is no fallback.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'An ammonia call states a domestic base price of 2 US$ per MMBtu (a stated probe) and a CMPP of 500, so the formula gives exactly 2. What does the engine return?', '["2.000000, held at the ceiling", "0.900000, the floor, since a formula price that reaches the ceiling drops to the floor", "A refusal, since the formula must fall strictly below the domestic base price of the call", "2.000000, with no hold"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ceiling holds the price only when the formula is above the domestic base price, so a formula exactly equal to it stands, and the engine returns 2.000000 with its held label none. Calling it held misreads the boundary. Nothing drops a price to the floor from above. A formula equal to the stated base is allowed.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'For methanol, a month-end price of 225 US$ per tonne takes the Fourth Schedule result to exactly 0.9. How is that month priced?', '["0.900000, held at the floor", "A refusal", "0.900000, the formula''s own figure with no hold", "1.000000, since a price that touches the floor is lifted to the National Reference Price of the Fourth Schedule"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EPF is (225 - 250) / 250 = -0.100000 and the formula gives 0.900000, which is not below the floor, so the engine returns it with its held label none; its reason reads "the formula gives 0.9, inside the floor 0.9" and the stated base price. The floor holds a price only when the formula is below it. A formula at the floor is a valid result. Nothing lifts a price to the NRP.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'Urea is priced for a gas based industry, but nobody enters the month-end product price. Which message comes back?', '["A price at the NRP of 1", "cmpp must be stated: the average current month end product price in US$ per tonne (Fourth Schedule); got nothing", "A price at the floor of US$0.90 per MMBtu, the lowest the Act allows a gas based industry to pay for its gas", "product must be given only for sector ''gas-based-industry''"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine refuses the call by name, since the Fourth Schedule price cannot be computed without the product price. It holds no default CMPP, so it never falls back on the NRP or the floor. The message about product is its refusal of a product stated for a sector other than the gas based industries.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'How does regulation 9 of the Domestic Gas Delivery Obligation Regulations 2022 define the domestic base price?', '["As US$3.50 per MMBtu", "As the price the Commission identifies on the national supply curve under regulation 5(2) each year", "As the power sector price plus the transport tariff from the delivery point to the facilities of the wholesale buyer", "As the price determined pursuant to the Act''s Third Schedule"]'::jsonb, 3, 'r.9 reads "“domestic base price” is the price for marketable natural gas determined pursuant to the Third Schedule to the Act ;". US$3.50 per MMBtu is the penalty rate of r.6(1). The supply curve of r.5(2) finds the lowest domestic gas supply price for the strategic sectors, which is a different quantity. Transport is added for a delivered price under s.167(8), outside the definition.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Over-delivery: 1200.000000 reaches the strategic sectors against an allocation of 1000.000000. What does the engine say?', '["The obligation met, with no penalty", "A credit of 200.000000 carried into next year''s obligation", "A penalty", "The obligation met, with the 200.000000 over-delivery counted toward the lessee''s voluntary contracts for deemed fulfilment next year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s reason reads "delivered 1200 meets the obligation 1000", and the penalty is 0.000000. The obligation is a stated input each year, so nothing is carried. Only gas not delivered is penalised. Over-delivery does not turn into voluntary contracts.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'A golden lessee delivers exactly its obligation of 1000.000000. What does the engine report?', '["A penalty of 3.500000 applies", "It is deemed fulfilled under s.110(2)(a), the provision for deliveries equal to the obligation", "It meets the obligation, with no export restriction", "It meets it, with the restriction flagged"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s reason reads "delivered 1000 meets the obligation 1000", with nothing undelivered, nothing penalised and no export restriction. Delivery equal to the obligation leaves nothing undelivered. Deemed fulfilment under s.110(2)(a) turns on voluntary contracts, and deliveries play no part in it. The restriction is reported only when a quantity is penalised.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'A signed agreement states a penalty rate of exactly 3.5 per MMBtu, and 400.000000 is undelivered. What rate basis does the engine state?', '["The Act''s own US$3.50, since an agreement rate equal to the minimum is treated as silent", "The agreement''s rate of 3.5, at or above the r.6(2) minimum", "A refusal", "A rate of 5"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s basis reads "the agreement''s rate 3.5 per MMBtu (s.110(8) proviso), at or above the US$3.50 minimum of r.6(2)", and the penalty is 1400.000000. The minimum is inclusive, so an equal rate is the agreement''s own. Nothing is refused. 5 is the rate of a different agreement.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'Section 110(13) of the Petroleum Industry Act 2021 makes a producer-customer of the domestic gas aggregator pay compensation to a customer-client for loss from a default in supply. What does the engine compute for it?', '["The compensation at US$3.50 per MMBtu", "The compensation at the agreement rate", "A deduction from the penalty", "Nothing; it is a concept the engine states it does not compute"]'::jsonb, 3, 's.110(13) sits in the engine''s list of rules it names as uncomputed, beside the Regulations'' 90-day investigation. The compensation is a loss suffered by a customer-client, and the Act prints no rate for it, so the engine does not apply the penalty rate or an agreement rate to it or offset one against the other.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'Why is 688800.000000 excused in the Ekene power plant''s synthetic 2028 allocation?', '["The fixture states force majeure, s.110(10)(a)", "The engine matches it to the deficiency", "The fixture states the purchaser could not accept, s.110(10)(b)", "The gas could not be transported, s.110(10)(c)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fixture''s note reads "synthetic allocation; the plant outage of 2028 is the purchaser''s inability to accept (s.110(10)(b))", and the engine''s reason reads "688800 is excused: the purchaser could not accept the allocated volumes (s.110(10)(b))". The excuse is a stated input on its stated ground; the engine does not read it off the take-or-pay ledger, and the fixture states neither force majeure nor a transport failure.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'With no agreement rate and no adjusted rate stated, what rate basis does the engine print for the power plant''s 2028 obligation penalty?', '["US$3.50 per MMBtu not delivered (s.110(8); DGDO Regulations 2022 r.6(1))", "US$1.25 per MMBtu, the power plant''s stated seller shortfall rate", "US$0.50 per MMBtu, the adder of s.167(6)", "A rate the engine derives from the take-or-pay price of the year, so that the penalty tracks the value of the gas"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s basis prints the Act''s rate with both citations: "US$3.50 per MMBtu not delivered (s.110(8); DGDO Regulations 2022 r.6(1))". 1.25 is the fixture''s synthetic contract rate for seller shortfall damages. The US$0.50 adder sets the commercial sector price. The engine derives no rate from a price.'),
  ('gsa', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'Under s.110(15) of the Petroleum Industry Act 2021, what does an approval to supply natural gas for export projects require?', '["Payment of the penalty", "Prior compliance by the lessee with its domestic gas delivery obligation", "A signed wholesale agreement", "An export price above the base price"]'::jsonb, 1, 's.110(15) reads "An approval for the supply of natural gas for export projects shall, from the effective date, be subject to prior compliance by the lessee with its domestic gas delivery obligation." Paying a penalty is not compliance. A signed agreement sets the penalty rate under s.110(8); it does not stand in for compliance. The Act sets no export price test here.');
